  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Before you start: set up for a smooth game</h2>
    <p>
      The best twists fall flat if the basics are messy. A little preparation means you spend the party playing, not
      sorting out tickets.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Print tickets the day before.</strong> Our <a href="/tambola-ticket-generator/">ticket generator</a> makes
        proper tickets, 6 or 12 to an A4 page, and can print each guest’s name on her ticket so nobody mixes them up.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Decide the prize list in advance.</strong> Write it on a card or a whiteboard: prize name, order and what
        the winner gets.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pick a caller.</strong> The host often calls, but then she can’t play. A
        <a href="/tambola-number-caller/">talking number caller</a> on a phone or TV lets everyone play, including the host.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Agree on bogey rules.</strong> Is a wrong claim just a laugh, or does it cost a forfeit? Decide before the
        first call. Our <a href="/guides/how-to-play-tambola/">tambola rules guide</a> explains the common options.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>New prize patterns to try</h2>
    <p>
      Adding two or three new patterns to the usual early five, lines, corners and full house is the easiest way to
      liven things up. Swap them each month so regulars always have something new to chase.
    </p>
    <h3>1. Star</h3>
    <p>
      The four corners plus the middle number of the middle row. It is a little harder than corners, so it usually
      lands in the middle of the game when attention starts to drift.
    </p>
    <h3>2. Pyramid</h3>
    <p>
      One number in the top row (the middle one), three in the middle row and all five in the bottom row. It takes
      a while, which makes it a good second-half prize.
    </p>
    <h3>3. Breakfast, lunch and dinner</h3>
    <p>
      Three prizes in one. Breakfast is every number in columns 1 to 3 (numbers 1 to 29), lunch is columns 4 to 6 (30 to
      59) and dinner is columns 7 to 9 (60 to 90). Perfect if you serve a meal in courses at the party.
    </p>
    <h3>4. Odd one out</h3>
    <p>
      Win by marking every odd number on your ticket, or every even one. Players have to look at their tickets in a new
      way, which causes lots of last-second gasps.
    </p>
    <h3>5. Lucky number</h3>
    <p>
      Before the game, the host writes a secret number on a slip and seals it. When that number is called, she opens
      the slip, and everyone who has it on their ticket shares a small prize. Nobody can plan for it, so it is pure fun.
    </p>
    <h3>6. Unlucky last</h3>
    <p>
      After the full house, the player with the fewest numbers marked gets a consolation prize. It turns the worst
      ticket of the day into a reason to cheer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <AdSlot name="inGuide" />

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Theme your tambola</h2>
    <p>
      A theme ties the party together and makes photos more fun. Match the tickets, prizes and calls to the occasion.
    </p>
    <h3>7. Festival specials</h3>
    <p>
      For Diwali, name the prizes after the festival: “Diya line”, “Rangoli corners”, “Lakshmi full house”. For Holi,
      give each line a colour. For Christmas and New Year, try “Santa’s sack” for the full house and a countdown round
      where the last ten numbers are called in a dramatic slow voice.
    </p>
    <h3>8. Bollywood tambola</h3>
    <p>
      Instead of a plain call, the caller hums or acts out a famous song that has the number in its lyrics or title, and
      players must guess the number. It slows the game a little, so play it for one round, not every round.
    </p>
    <h3>9. Dress-code bonus</h3>
    <p>
      Set a dress code, such as “something red” or “retro”, and give the best-dressed guest an extra ticket. More
      tickets means more chances, so people make an effort.
    </p>
    <h3>10. Memory lane calls</h3>
    <p>
      Ask each member before the party for a number that means something to her: a wedding year, a child’s birthday, a
      house number. When that number comes up, she tells the group the story in one sentence. It is a lovely way for a
      group to get to know each other better.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Twists for the game itself</h2>
    <h3>11. Speed round</h3>
    <p>
      After the main game, play a quick round with only early five and one line, with numbers called every five
      seconds. It works well at the end of the party when people are getting ready to leave.
    </p>
    <h3>12. Swap tickets</h3>
    <p>
      Halfway through the game, everyone passes her ticket to the person on her left. The marked numbers stay. It is
      silly, slightly chaotic and always gets a laugh, especially when someone hands over a ticket one number away from a
      line.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Small prize ideas</h2>
    <p>
      Prizes do not need to be expensive. Something thoughtful or funny beats something costly, and keeping prizes small
      keeps the game friendly. A few ideas that work well at kitty parties:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Treats:</strong> a box of chocolates, homemade sweets, a jar of cookies or a fancy tea pack.</li>
      <li><strong>Home and self-care:</strong> a scented candle, hand cream, a small plant, a pretty mug or a bath bomb set.</li>
      <li><strong>Useful bits:</strong> a recipe notebook, a kitchen gadget, a phone stand or a cute keychain.</li>
      <li><strong>Fun awards:</strong> a paper crown for the full house winner, or a rotating trophy that the winner brings back next month.</li>
      <li><strong>Privileges:</strong> the winner picks next month’s theme, menu or venue.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="tip">
      <strong>Keep it friendly:</strong> tambola is a game of chance played for fun. Give gifts, not cash, and keep
      prizes modest so the game never feels like gambling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Hosting tips from experienced kitty hosts</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Seat new members next to regulars</strong> so they can learn the patterns without feeling lost.</li>
      <li><strong>Give each guest two tickets</strong> if your group is small (under eight). The game moves faster and more people win something.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep a board visible.</strong> A TV or laptop showing the called numbers saves “What was the last number?” every few minutes.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan 30 to 45 minutes</strong> for the main game with extra patterns, and serve snacks before, not during, so hands are free to mark tickets.</li>
      <li><strong>Take a photo of every winner</strong> with her prize. It makes a great group chat post after the party.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel faq">
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>How many tickets should each guest get?</summary>
      <p>
        One each is standard. With fewer than eight guests, two each keeps the game moving. Experienced players can
        manage three, but new players usually struggle with more than two.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can we play kitty party tambola online?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. Run the caller on a shared screen in your video call and send each member a ticket, either a printed PDF or
        a link to a ticket on her phone. The caller page explains how room links work.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How many prizes should we have?</summary>
      <p>
        Six to nine prizes suits a group of 10 to 20 players: early five, three lines, corners, one or two special
        patterns and the full house. More prizes than that and the game drags.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
